{"name":"Pastel Grey Green","hex":"#bccbb9","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pastel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several subdued tints of colors, usually associated with pink, peach, yellow, green, blue, and lavender.","A drawing made with any of those colors."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional dish in various Latin American countries and in Indonesia, resembling a tamale, pasty, or calzone.","A Filipino stew made with vegetables, sausages, and chicken or other meat in a creamy sauce."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pastel"},{"term":"grey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray.","Synonym of coloured (pertaining to the mixed race of black and white)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of gray."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pastel","blocks":[{"text":"A pastel is an art medium that consists of powdered pigment and a binder. It can exist in a variety of forms, including a stick, a square, a pebble, and a pan of color, among other forms. The pigments used in pastels are similar to those used to produce some other colored visual arts media, such as oil paints; the binder is of a neutral hue and low saturation. The color effect of pastels is closer to the natural dry pigments than that of any other process."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg/330px-William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastel"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg/500px-William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg/1280px-William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg/500px-William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"William Merritt Chase, Study of Flesh Color and Gold, 1888, National Gallery of Art","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliam_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Louis15-1.jpg/500px-Louis15-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Louis15-1.jpg/1280px-Louis15-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Louis15-1.jpg/500px-Louis15-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Maurice Quentin de La Tour, a bravura pastel portrait of Louis XV, 1748","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALouis15-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Commercial_pastels.JPG/500px-Commercial_pastels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Commercial_pastels.JPG/1280px-Commercial_pastels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.